<Base
  title="About · Andreas Jackson"
  description="About Andreas (AJ) Jackson: Berkeley Haas grad, EY analyst, founder of NotTomorrow, operator of a short-term rental business, and founder of a youth soccer nonprofit."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose-wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Hi, I'm AJ.</h1>
      <p>
        I'm Andreas Jackson, an analyst and builder in the San Francisco Bay
        Area. I studied business at UC Berkeley's Haas School (class of 2025,
        GPA 3.7, Dean's List), spent three years running strategy engagements
        for clients like the Gates Foundation, IKEA, and T-Mobile, worked
        inside early-stage startups in London and Tokyo, and now work in EY's
        financial services assurance practice.
      </p>
      <p>
        Outside the day job I like building things that have to survive contact
        with reality: <a href="/projects/nottomorrow">NotTomorrow</a>, a SaaS
        product with paying users, and
        <a href="/projects/dune-lakes-business">Dune Lakes</a>, a short-term
        rental business I help run. Most recently I ran
        <a href="/projects/scaffolding-vs-scale">an empirical study</a> on how
        AI models behave on real occupational work, which taught me more about
        research than any coursework did.
      </p>
      <p>
        What ties it together: I like owning a number. Whether it's an audit
        workpaper, a partner-scoring model, or a night's ADR, I want to know how
        it was built and be able to defend it.
      </p>

      <h2>What I care about</h2>
      <p>
        In 2020 I founded a youth soccer nonprofit in Santa Ynez, which I still
        run. We won charitable grants for uniforms and equipment for a local
        high school team, and we sponsor a club team so kids get recruited on
        talent instead of on whether their family can afford the fees. It's
        small, and it's the thing I'm most consistently glad I did.
      </p>
      <p>
        That thread runs through a lot of my work. The Gates Foundation
        engagement, The Assistance Fund, the nonprofit clients at ABA. I'd like
        the long arc of my career to leave things better than I found them, and
        I think the way I contribute is by building useful things and being
        rigorous about whether they actually work.
      </p>

      <h2>What I'm looking for</h2>
      <p>
        A full-time role where analytical rigor and shipping speed both matter:
        AI implementation and applied AI, product management, data and
        analytics, strategy and operations, or strategic finance. Anywhere in
        the Bay Area works for me, San Francisco through the Peninsula and
        South Bay, and I'm also open to Santa Barbara or remote.
      </p>

      <h2>Off the clock</h2>
      <p>
        Chess, skiing, soccer, a weekly economics newsletter, and following the
        semiconductor and data-center industry (yes, I've read Chip War). I also
        spend a lot of time experimenting with AI workflows, which you can read
        about in <a href="/how-i-work">how I work</a>.
      </p>

